the House Oversight Committee this  morning is investigating the Trump  administration's travel practices the  administration was already under fire  for travel by Health and Human Services  Secretary Tom Price and Treasury  secretary Steve Newton now CBS News has  learned the EPA paid tens of thousands  of dollars for administrator Scott  Pruitt to take non commercial flights  Julianna Goldman broke the story this  week and has new details from Washington  Julianna good morning good morning well  former government officials and ethics  watchdogs say the non commercial air  travel by Trump administration officials  is unprecedented documents obtained by  CBS News show the Environmental  Protection Agency and its administrator  Scott Pruitt have spent more than 58,000  dollars in non commercial travel costs  since February on July 27th EPA  Administrator Scott Pruitt flew on a  private plane from his hometown of Tulsa  to an event in Guymon Oklahoma then he  boarded the same 12 seat Learjet and  flew to Oklahoma City records obtained  by CBS News show that in total he spent  83 minutes in the air costing taxpayers  more than 14 thousand dollars the EPA  says the flight was chartered through  the Interior Department as part of an  interagency program there were no  commercial flights available and the  drive was 5 hours each way process rule  of law and cooperative federalism that's  going to be the heart of how we do  business at the EPA in total Pruett has  spent nearly $60,000 on non commercial  travel since February according to  information obtained by CBS News and  documents provided to Senator Sheldon  Whitehouse who requested the details  last month in June tax payers shelled  out $36,000 when Pruitt flew on an Air  Force jet from Cincinnati to New York  where he caught a flight to Italy for an  international summit in August Pruitt  took a $5,700 air charter between Denver  and Durango Colorado in both cases the  EPA says commercial travel would have  prevented Pruett from attending official  events on Wednesday President Trump was  asked to address travel costs of other  members of his cabinet including  Treasury secretary Steve Newton's use of  military and government planes and 400  thousand dollars wrapped up by his HHS  Secretary Tom price inspectors general  at the EPA the Treasury Department and  the Department of Health and Human  Services are now all looking into air  travel habits the EPA says its ethics  office approved all of Pruitt's  non-commercial travel and Charlie a  spokesperson says Pruitt has taken one  private charter and three government  flights and there have been no  additional non commercial flights  Julianna thanks
 

